       checked first (invalid-batch-item, that item's index). So a malformed
       item is reported at its own position even when an earlier, well-formed
       item would have failed on its own business rules -- the same guarantee
       G3 gave, now decided on one left-to-right pass.
    3. An item whose structure is sound is then judged by its operation ID
       before any business rule: a repeat of an earlier ID with identical
       request data is a retry, which contributes no result entry and no units
       of its own -- the first item already answered it. A repeat with
       different data is `conflicting-operation` at the later item's index.
    4. Only a genuinely new ID runs the G2 validation and computation. A real
       failure here ends the batch with that item's own code and index.
